Q: Navbar fixed left - Bootstrap A: <p>try adding these things.  Add extra CSS to modify existing class.</p>

<p>Add this extra css:-</p>

<pre><code>&lt;style&gt;
.navbar-fixed-left {
width: 140px;
position: fixed;
border-radius: 0;
height: 100%;
}

.navbar-fixed-left .navbar-nav &gt; li {
float: none;  /* Cancel default li float: left */
width: 139px;
}

.navbar-fixed-left + .container {
padding-left: 160px;
}

/* On using dropdown menu (To right shift popuped) */
.navbar-fixed-left .navbar-nav &gt; li &gt; .dropdown-menu {
margin-top: -50px;
margin-left: 140px;
}
&lt;/style&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: Best way to update a clock every second in Linux A: <p>The real problem is not the context switch itself. The kernel is designed to handle sleeping processes efficiently. The bigger issue is spawning a new <code>date</code> process every second and losing timing accuracy because every command startup adds some delay.</p>
<p>A cleaner approach is to keep one process alive, let it sleep, and wake it up only when needed. The scheduler handles the waiting, so the CPU is not being wasted.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;time.h&gt;
#include &lt;unistd.h&gt;

void clock_loop(void)
{
    while (1) {
        printf(&quot;\r%s&quot;, ctime(&amp;(time_t){time(NULL)}));
        fflush(stdout);
        sleep(1);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<blockquote>
<p>This is basically how you would normally do it in C: one process, no busy loop, no unnecessary process creation. The program sleeps most of the time, and the kernel wakes it up when the timer expires.</p>
</blockquote>
